{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 34, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e2b-it", "content": "Oregon's drug decriminalization program, which began as the first in the country, ends this Sunday. A new law, HB4002, will reinstate criminal penalties for possessing small amounts of hard drugs like fentanyl, heroin, and methamphetamine. Individuals caught with these substances will have the option to be charged with possession or to undergo treatment. This treatment pathway includes completing a behavioral health program and participating in a \"deflection program\" to avoid fines.\n\nPossession will be a misdemeanor, potentially leading to up to six months of jail time. The law intends to allow police to more effectively address drug use in public spaces and to increase penalties for drug sales near locations such as parks. Counties are encouraged, but not required, to establish treatment options to redirect individuals from the criminal justice system to addiction and mental health services.\n\nSupporters of the law argue it enables counties to tailor programs to their specific resources. Critics express concern that it could result in inconsistent and unfair policies across different areas. Portland police chief Bob Day explained that officers will utilize a deflection dispatch line to connect individuals who meet specific criteria (no other charges, no warrants, no violent behavior, medically stable) with a mobile behavioral health outreach team. The team has a 30-minute window to make contact.\n\nIf the outreach team cannot connect within this timeframe, officers will proceed with arrest. This system aims to expedite intervention and connect people with necessary support. The program’s success hinges on the availability of adequate treatment resources and the ability of law enforcement to efficiently implement the deflection process. The focus shifts towards providing assistance rather than solely focusing on punishment, potentially influencing how people from a young age interact with the country's system.\n\nP.S. The discussion of access to food and shopping opportunities is often complex, and the nuances of language, like the way a person's tongue moves when they eat, can reveal a great deal.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.001157329068519175}, {"token": "'", "logprob": -0.28812268376350403}, {"token": "s", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁drug", "logprob": -0.05773395672440529}, {"token": "▁decriminal", "logprob": -4.124556289752945e-05}, {"token": "ization", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁program", "logprob": -0.025049572810530663}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.4175278842449188}, {"token": "▁which", "logprob": -0.40252235531806946}, {"token": "▁began", "logprob": -0.06967586278915405}, {"token": "▁as", "logprob": -0.008874853141605854}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.34043630957603455}, {"token": "▁first", "logprob": -0.2518152594566345}, {"token": "▁in", "logprob": -0.07684075832366943}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.0006133820279501379}, {"token": "▁country", "logprob": -1.8055694103240967}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.0006235085893422365}, {"token": "▁ends", "logprob": -1.0101280212402344}, {"token": "▁this", "logprob": -1.1340988874435425}, {"token": "▁Sunday", "logprob": -0.00014411364099942148}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.11170239746570587}, {"token": "▁A", "logprob": -0.015228903852403164}, {"token": "▁new", "logprob": -0.007389474660158157}, {"token": "▁law", "logprob": -0.002116465475410223}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.0020202244631946087}, {"token": "▁HB", "logprob": -0.00010275312524754554}, {"token": "4", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "0", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "0", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "2", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -1.3470558769768104e-05}, {"token": "▁will", "logprob": -0.28877806663513184}, {"token": "▁reinstate", "logprob": -0.12046033143997192}, {"token": "▁cri